Summary of Work Completed
-------------------------
A full gravity-only N-body simulation and analysis pipeline was built from scratch
in Python. The pipeline spans initial condition generation, numerical integration,
statistical phase-space analysis, and physically interpretable diagnostics.

The work completed today is equivalent to a full research sprint and would normally
be spread across multiple development sessions.

Detailed Work Log
-----------------

1. Research Framing and Problem Definition
   - Defined a project scope that is:
     • Scientifically legitimate
     • Computationally non-trivial
     • Feasible without proprietary data or supercomputing resources
   - Selected the MW–M31 merger as the physical system.
   - Identified a novel analysis angle: dynamical irreversibility and disk heating,
     rather than only merger timescales or morphology.

2. Physical Assumptions and Model Design
   - Chose a collisionless, gravity-only framework.
   - Explicitly excluded gas physics to control complexity.
   - Modeled each galaxy using:
     • An exponential stellar disk
     • A Hernquist dark matter halo
   - Defined clear component tags for later analysis:
     0 = Milky Way disk
     1 = Milky Way halo
     2 = M31 disk
     3 = M31 halo

3. Initial Condition Generation
   - Implemented a Hernquist sphere generator for dark matter halos.
   - Implemented an exponential disk generator with rotational support.
   - Assigned particle masses consistently to conserve total mass.
   - Offset M31 spatially and kinematically to create an approaching merger setup.
   - Ensured all particles carry full phase-space information and metadata.

4. Numerical Integration (N-body Solver)
   - Implemented a leapfrog (kick–drift–kick) integrator.
   - Implemented softened Newtonian gravity to avoid numerical divergences.
   - Selected timestep and softening parameters appropriate for galaxy-scale dynamics.
   - Built a loop to evolve the system for multiple gigayears.
   - Stored full snapshots at each timestep for post-processing.

5. Phase-Space Representation
   - Designed a coarse-graining procedure for 6D phase space
     (x, y, z, vx, vy, vz).
   - Used multidimensional histograms to approximate the distribution function.
   - Normalized phase-space densities for information-theoretic analysis.

6. Irreversibility Metric (Core Analysis Contribution)
   - Implemented a time-reversal comparison between early and late snapshots.
   - Used Kullback–Leibler divergence to quantify time asymmetry.
   - Computed irreversibility separately for:
     • Stellar disks
     • Dark matter halos
   - Established a quantitative way to identify when the merger becomes
     dynamically irreversible.

7. Disk Heating Diagnostics
   - Implemented vertical velocity dispersion (σ_z²) as a heating metric.
   - Tracked the evolution of disk heating across the merger timeline.
   - Linked heating growth to irreversibility onset.

8. Visualization and Outputs
   - Generated time-series plots of:
     • Disk vs halo irreversibility
     • Disk vertical heating
   - Produced figures suitable for reports, presentations, or GitHub repositories.
   - Ensured outputs are interpretable without requiring domain-specific explanation.

9. Documentation and Reporting
   - Wrote a formal project summary text file describing the pipeline.
   - Structured explanations to align with OPT reporting expectations.
   - Framed the work in a way that demonstrates:
     • Independent research capability
     • Numerical and statistical sophistication
     • Physical insight

